{"given": "Pengcheng", "family": "Wen", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "5801", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 29]], "date-time": "2026-06-29T23:02:28Z", "timestamp": 1782774148000}, "score": 33.000153, "degree": ["M.Phil."], "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://hdl.handle.net/1783.1/172505"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[null]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.14711/thesis-hdl172505"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 26]], "date-time": "2026-02-26T19:16:30Z", "timestamp": 1772133390640, "version": "3.50.1"}, "description": "Supplemental Information for 10.1021/jacs.5c02097", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "American Chemical Society (ACS)", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "DOI": "10.1021/jacs.5c02097.s001", "type": "component", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 7, 3]], "date-time": "2025-07-03T10:20:38Z", "timestamp": 1751538038000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Generative Design of Functional Metal Complexes Utilizing the Internal Knowledge and Reasoning Capability of Large Language Models"], "prefix": "10.1021", "member": "316", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 9, 10]], "date-time": "2025-09-10T07:55:49Z", "timestamp": 1757490949000}, "score": 31.973204, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://pubs.acs.org/doi/suppl/10.1021/jacs.5c02097/suppl_file/ja5c02097_si_001.pdf"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[null]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1021/jacs.5c02097.s001", "relation": {"is-component-of": [{"id-type": "doi", "id": "10.1021/jacs.5c02097", "asserted-by": "object"}]}}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 11]], "date-time": "2026-03-11T18:42:26Z", "timestamp": 1773254546899, "version": "3.50.1"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 10]]}, "group-title": "Computer Science and Mathematics", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "MDPI AG", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 10]], "date-time": "2026-03-10T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1773100800000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "accepted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 9]]}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) face a fundamental challenge in balancing efficient \"fast thinking\" with accurate \"slow thinking,\" often struggling to adaptively trigger deeper reasoning without incurring significant computational overhead. This paper introduces \\( \\textit{MetaThink (MT)} \\), a novel inference-time adaptive refinement framework designed to imbue LRMs with conditional self-correction capabilities, without requiring any additional training. \\( \\textit{MetaThink} \\) operates by an initial \"fast thinking\" phase, followed by a lightweight self-monitoring mechanism that assesses confidence through uncertainty markers. When low confidence or potential errors are detected, a refinement token triggers a targeted \"slow thinking\" phase, guided by domain-specific prompts. This allows the model to introspectively review and correct its reasoning, culminating in a more accurate final answer. Our comprehensive evaluation across diverse and challenging benchmarks\u2014spanning mathematical reasoning, code generation, and scientific problem-solving tasks\u2014demonstrates that \\( \\textit{MetaThink} \\) consistently achieves substantial and robust improvements in Pass@1 accuracy. Crucially, these gains are realized while maintaining competitive or even improved inference efficiency, outperforming existing inference-time baselines. Our findings underscore that \\( \\textit{MetaThink} \\) offers an effective, training-free approach to enhance the reliability and accuracy of LRMs in complex reasoning tasks by striking a superior balance between performance and efficiency.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.20944/preprints202603.0750.v1", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 11]], "date-time": "2026-03-11T01:09:54Z", "timestamp": 1773191394000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["MetaThink: Empowering Large Reasoning Models with Adaptive Self-Correction at Inference Time"], "prefix": "10.20944", "author": [{"given": "Jiarui", "family": "Qi", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}, {"given": "Haoyu", "family": "Bian", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}], "member": "1968", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 11]], "date-time": "2026-03-11T01:10:47Z", "timestamp": 1773191447000}, "score": 30.803925, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.preprints.org/manuscript/202603.0750/v1"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 10]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202603.0750.v1", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 10]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 11, 20]], "date-time": "2025-11-20T06:19:45Z", "timestamp": 1763619585764, "version": "3.45.0"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2025]]}, "group-title": "SSRN", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Elsevier BV", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.5774579", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 11, 20]], "date-time": "2025-11-20T05:43:11Z", "timestamp": 1763617391000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["When Correct Isn't Enough: Deconstructing Legal Causal Reasoning Capability in Large Language Models"], "prefix": "10.2139", "author": [{"given": "Jee Won", "family": "Park", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}, {"given": "Sungmi", "family": "\u200dPark", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}], "member": "78", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 11, 20]], "date-time": "2025-11-20T05:43:11Z", "timestamp": 1763617391000}, "score": 30.610744, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.ssrn.com/abstract=5774579"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5774579", "published": {"date-parts": [[2025]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 12]], "date-time": "2026-06-12T17:00:30Z", "timestamp": 1781283630303, "version": "3.54.1"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 5]]}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE)", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 5]], "date-time": "2026-03-05T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1772668800000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a powerful paradigm for combining large language models with external knowledge sources to produce accurate, context-aware, and verifiable outputs. In this survey, we focus on an advanced instantiation of this paradigm, referred to as Deep RAG, which integrates reasoning, memory, and multi-step planning into end-to-end research workflows. We provide a structured overview of Deep RAG systems, covering key components such as query planning, information retrieval, memory management, and answer generation, as well as practical optimization techniques including supervised fine-tuning, iterative self-evolving, and agentic reinforcement learning. We analyze different planning strategies, adaptive retrieval mechanisms, conflict resolution methods, and multimodal synthesis, highlighting both their strengths and limitations. By categorizing approaches according to workflow types, reasoning strategies, and optimization paradigms, we clarify the current landscape and identify open challenges for evaluation, scalability, and multimodal reasoning. This survey aims to provide a comprehensive roadmap for designing flexible, reliable, and intelligent Deep RAG systems capable of performing complex, real-world research tasks with minimal human supervision.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.36227/techrxiv.177272838.89432844/v1", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 5]], "date-time": "2026-03-05T16:33:10Z", "timestamp": 1772728390000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["A Survey of (Deep RAG) Deep Retrieval Augmented Generation and Reasoning in Large Language Models"], "prefix": "10.36227", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3758-4041", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Lihui", "family": "Liu", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Wayne State University"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "263", "link": [{"URL": "https://www.techrxiv.org/doi/pdf/10.36227/techrxiv.177272838.89432844/v1", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 12]], "date-time": "2026-06-12T16:31:45Z", "timestamp": 1781281905000}, "score": 30.450407, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.techrxiv.org/doi/full/10.36227/techrxiv.177272838.89432844/v1"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 5]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.177272838.89432844/v1", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 5]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 13]], "date-time": "2026-08-13T10:32:55Z", "timestamp": 1786617175104, "version": "build-2736575974"}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Hill Publishing Group Inc.", "issue": "2", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "short-container-title": ["JAMC"], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 22]]}, "DOI": "10.26855/jamc.2026.06.004", "type": "journal-artic